title: Making a bus wider
mode: vhdl
chapter: Buses and numbers
check: behaves
---- intro
To put a 4-bit number into an 8-bit bus, add zeros at the top: `"0000" & a`. The number stays the same: 0101 and 00000101 are both 5. This is called **zero extension**.

A bus written as a constant uses double quotes, `"0000"`, one character per bit.
---- task
Make the 8-bit `y` hold the 4-bit number `a`, with zeros in the top four bits.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity widen is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(7 downto 0)
  );
end widen;

architecture rtl of widen is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity widen is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    y : out std_logic_vector(7 downto 0)
  );
end widen;

architecture rtl of widen is
begin
    y <= "0000" & a;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Four zeros, joined in front of `a`.
---- hint2
`y <= "0000" & a;`
---- solution
`y <= "0000" & a;` The four zeros become bits 7 to 4.
---- done
VHDL will not let you put 4 bits into 8 without saying how: the widths must match exactly. It catches a lot of mistakes.
